  "knowledge_gaps": [
    "The report specifies right-padding without verifying the baseline convention. SASRec padding and absolute position indices can confound deletion effects. Target alignment must also guarantee that no interaction after the selected prediction position enters its history.",
    "Availability-constrained shuffling lacks a reproducible construction. Batches contain no user IDs or timestamps, so histories cannot necessarily be matched unambiguously to public timestamps. Training-set first appearance is only an availability proxy.",
    "A local perturbation RNG does not isolate diagnosis from training: iterating the training loader can advance sampler, worker, and negative-sampling state. Perturbation counts, deletion locations, shuffle repetitions, aggregation weights, and runtime limits remain unspecified.",
    "Pairwise training sensitivity does not directly measure top-10 specificity or improvement in macro validation HR@10. The potential score of 8 is speculative, and implementation difficulty of 4 may underestimate alignment and reproducibility requirements."
  ],
  "follow_up_queries": [
    {
      "reason": "Resolve padding, position, and causal-target alignment before interpreting perturbation effects.",
      "query": "In the original SASRec implementation and sequential recommendation perturbation studies, how are left-padding, absolute positional embeddings, causal masks, and shifted next-item targets handled when deleting or shuffling history? What controls distinguish content dependence from sequence-length and position changes while keeping prediction targets fixed?"
    },
    {
      "reason": "Determine whether the proposed constrained shuffle is implementable and what its measurements can establish.",
      "query": "Liu et al. RecSys 2025 DOI 10.1145/3705328.3748042: what exact algorithm and timestamp information are required for item-availability-constrained history shuffling? How are repeated items, tied timestamps, truncated histories, and training-only first-observation proxies handled, and what confounding remains?"
    },
    {
      "reason": "Make diagnostics reproducible without changing subsequent training, and define a credible calibration protocol.",
      "query": "For PyTorch sequential recommendation diagnostics, how can a separate diagnostic DataLoader preserve training sampler, worker, negative-sampling, and Python/NumPy/Torch RNG states? How should paired history-perturbation effects be aggregated across users and four datasets, then calibrated against fixed-candidate validation HR@10 across checkpoints without selecting diagnostic thresholds on the same validation results?"
    }
  ]
}
